A||||20||Gokart||Keep the fan on for cooling?||||5||
Z||000000||Gokart||07-26-2000||06:41 AM||||I wonder if it's a good thing to do? Mines been running cooler since I installed the 180 thermo, but still climbs up there on a hot day stuck in traffic. Goes right down when I turn the a/c fan on. I'm out of freon right now so using it up isn't a issue. I'm wondering if it's better for the engine just to keep the fan running constantly....at least for the summer. Tia for replies.<P>------------------<BR> Marc                     87GT||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FieroGTRacingInc||07-26-2000||07:17 AM||||I've had mine direct wired to the ignition,so when the var is on ,the fan is alway's on....  my a/c is broke....  Maybe this will help you.     steve||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FieroGTRacingInc||07-26-2000||07:18 AM||||it's a car ... not a var...  lol||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||07-26-2000||09:19 AM||||I talked to a guy the other day with a beautiful little 84 coupe, who had hard wired the radiator fan to the blower fan switch, and remved the thermostat all together. He said it gives him total control over the fan, and that it works great. I question running the load of the radiator fan off the blower switch, but it's his mod, and he says it's great.<BR>Personally, I'd go to a boneyard, scam a used fan control switch (any car, any year) and wire the seperate one to the radiator fan. (have to make sure to get the resistor setup as well if necessary)<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||07-26-2000||09:21 AM||||If you want to keep your temp down a 180 thermo is a good idea, but you must also change your fan switch to a lower limit in the block as well.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||07-26-2000||01:30 PM||||Putting the hood vents/ducts in helps to aid cooling also. With the hood vents the heat from the radiator will still rise up and out the vents/ducts. Having the radiator fan on all the time sounds like you'll be replacing the fan motor more often. Removing the thermostat isn't such a good idea. During Highway driving the car could run too cool and play havoc with the ECM. Unless the ECM is reprogrammed you don't want to put in a thermostat below 180. Even with the Hypertech 165 thermostat they have it programmed into the chip.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Patrick||07-26-2000||04:29 PM||||<BR>Marc, there's no need to have the fan on ALL the time. Even on a hot day, you only really need it on when you're in stop and go traffic. As long as you're moving for any length of time, enough air should be getting pushed through the rad to keep everything cool enough. Just install a switch on the dash that grounds the fan when it's clicked on. It's real simple to hook up to the fan relay that's near the driver's side headlight. Even when this switch is not clicked on, the fan will STILL come on if and when the sensor reads that it's appropriate. My Fiero is set up this way and it works great. I agree with everything that Oreif said, especially about not taking the thermostat out. If you need any more details, such as which wire to splice into, just ask.||||reg||14
Z||000007||Standard||07-26-2000||04:58 PM||||I did what Patrick mentioned, wired a switch in to ground the fan.  Super easy to do and I like having control over the fan.. switch it on when I know I'm going to get stuck in traffic, turn it off when I get up to speed again.  Temp hasn't been above the half-way mark since.<P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 147,000 miles and counting!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Gokart||07-27-2000||06:32 AM||||I was also thinking by keeping the fan running constantly with the summer heat you know the alternator and coil will stay cooler with air shooting out at them through those aluminum pipes!?! Is there any other ill effects to be aware off besides eventually having to put in a new fan relay from overuse?<P>------------------<BR> Marc                     87GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||07-27-2000||12:35 PM||||If the majority of your driving is in heavy city traffic, then you could run it all the time. If a significant portion of your driving is on the highway, then wire in a switch or find a lower fan temp sensor. Running the fan all the time will probably cause it to wear out faster. How much faster, I don't know, but those fans are simple to remove and lots of cheap ones in the salvage yards. Why not go out now and grab a spare fan...just in case.||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||07-27-2000||03:32 PM||||when u turn on ur AC it makes fan run all the time. If ur ever going to fix ur AC its not a good idea to keep running compressor empty, its very hard on it. I fixed a slight run hot by using HiTech 160* stat and 175* fan switch. prob solved - with or without air seldom passes the first 1/4 mark even in 95* stop n go traffic runs perfect.<P>rg<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Gokart||07-27-2000||09:16 PM||||Thanks guys for the replies! Overheating isn't really an issue and I am on the highway more than stop and go traffic. I was just wondering if it was a GOOD thing to do to keep things cooler. I want my compressor to be working when I finally do get a freon refill so I'll use the fan accordingly <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> Marc                     87GT||||reg||7
Z||000012||EdsB52||07-28-2000||02:44 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Patrick:<BR><B><BR>Marc, there's no need to have the fan on ALL the time. Even on a hot day, you only really need it on when you're in stop and go traffic. As long as you're moving for any length of time, enough air should be getting pushed through the rad to keep everything cool enough. Just install a switch on the dash that grounds the fan when it's clicked on. It's real simple to hook up to the fan relay that's near the driver's side headlight. Even when this switch is not clicked on, the fan will STILL come on if and when the sensor reads that it's appropriate. My Fiero is set up this way and it works great. I agree with everything that Oreif said, especially about not taking the thermostat out. If you need any more details, such as which wire to splice into, just ask.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is exactly right. If you remove your thermostat, the engine will run cold most of the time. Engine tollerances and mixtures are established per a given, constant engine temp., so removal of the thermostat is a terrible idea. Splicing into the fan switch on the engine head, about 7 o'clock to the #4 cyl on 4bangers is the best way to do it. The fan has the hot wire going to the electric fan motor at all times, the fan switch at the #4 cyl acts as the ground when the engine reaches about 450 farenheit (kidding, but not far from it), so if you splice into the wire and run a switch to the dash, both you and the fan switch at the #4 cyl have control of the fan motor. It's also a bad idea to wire the fan motor hot all the time. The load it puts on the alternator and engine is obvious and noticeable. It will rob horsies from the already anemic 4 banger.||||reg||14
Z||000013||Patrick||07-28-2000||03:16 AM||||<BR>Ed, glad to see that you agree with my post <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">, but I'm a little surprised that you suggest to splice into the wiring at the fan switch on the motor. Isn't it a heck of a lot simpler to run wiring from the <I>front</I> of the car to the dash rather than from the <I>back</I>? Splicing into the fan relay in the front does not change at all how the fan switch normally works. Is there an advantage of doing it the way you suggest that I'm not aware of? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000014||EdsB52||07-28-2000||11:57 PM||||Actually Patrick, splicing into the wire at either location doesn't adversely affect operation of the fan. You still have the same control in either situation. I guess wire routing might be easier from the fan, but I simply chose to wire it from the rear, through the center support, and up under the dash to the manual switch. 6 of 1, half dozen of the other. ||||reg||6
Z||000015||WGraffiKs||07-29-2000||12:05 AM||||GoKart,<BR>If I'm not mistaken, the air for those tubes pointing at the HV components in the engine compartment comes from a small fan mounted under the carpet of the trunk. The rad. fan will not affect the airflow at those tubes.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Gokart||07-29-2000||06:14 AM||||But don't they only blow air out if the fan is on? I've never felt air coming out of the tubes unless I had the a/c on or the fan kicked on itself!||||reg||5
Z||000017||theogre||07-29-2000||12:21 PM||||I've covered a bunch of this in the cave..... Part of it depends on just what nose is on the car and the condition of it. If parts are missing undernieth it will screw up air flow, badly. In short wether or not you need it full time depends on your traffic. Here in the wilmington/philly area it's easier to lock it on in hot weather, but there's no need in cold except if traffic is clogged really bad.<P>correct. the aux fan for the alt and coils runs with the radiator fan. they are both run from the same circuit. <P>Yes, It would be posible to run them seperatly but may not be simple. You'd need to know about what amount of power the aux fan uses before deciding how to wire it. One option would be to set it up with an air temp thermostat so that when the engine compartment gets hot the aux fan comes on. (Engine compartment air temps can reach over 200F even on 60-70F days.) You'll need to use a thermostat and a relay if you do that. The thermostat contacts likely won't take allot of power thru them.<P><BR>------------------<BR>Lake Superior? Is that what I stepped in? Sorry.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||squid||07-30-2000||09:01 PM||||It gets very hot here in Atlanta. I have a toggle switch that is spliced into the wire from the fan switch so either one can cut the fan on. According to the Haynes manual, the fan switch doesnt cut on the fan until 235 degrees(!!!) on my 4 cylinder car. There are some GM cars that had switches that actuate the fan at 200 or 190 but I dont know what they are--probably '80's cars. The switch provides a ground to the relay.   ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Standard||07-31-2000||11:00 PM||||Has anyone thought about mounting some fans on the grills?  Could help to keep the compartment temp down, especially if you have a modded car.  shouldn't be too hard to find some fans that will work..<P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 148,000 miles and counting!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||lowCG||08-01-2000||01:14 AM||||Since the Fiero is a "bottom feeder",getting it's air primarily from beneath the car,and not through the tiny opening in front,it's good to have a large enough opening there.<BR>It's also helpful to have an exhaust opening in the hood,like many of us have done.<BR>From time to time,I check to see if parts of my radiator are plugged by checking to see if there are any spots that are getting hot,while the areas next to that are cold.This can only be done while the car is becoming warm.||||reg||1
